> 1) Core image files.
>
> Since P?S/8 is 4K-oriented, there is not a cross-system direct way to=20
> transfer extended memory [but it can be done indirectly].  The easy way=
=20
> that works easy is this:
>
> P?S/8 binary loading supports saving all of field 0 on the P?S/8 scratch=
=20
> area which is meaningful to a few groups of features and commands [but=20
> these groups are incompatible with each other, so you have to chose which=
=20
> purpose.  The one that applies is this:
>
> The P?S/8 BIN program is used to load slurp-format binary files.  These=
=20
> are in turn created either by assembling source files using P?S/8 PAL
>
> or
>
> The BIN system program has alternate utility modes to punch or read=20
> paper-tapes in either RIM or BIN format into either memory [in which case=
=20
> it is essentially analogous to the DEC paper-tape BIN and/or RIM loader] =
or=20
> to become the contents of slurp format binary files.
>
> Thus, BFL1 BFL2 BFL3 < /P /F /R  OOPS!  Start that command with BIN [or R=
=20
> BIN].  Those are the passed files and option switches, [but you have to=
=20
> have a program to pass them to!]
>

>
> This writes the paper-tape input to at most the three passed output files=
.=20
>  When the tape reaches the EOF, the last file is written out.  A file cou=
nt=20
> is always reported of what was used, in case too many were supplied to th=
e=20
> command processor.  If too few, an error message will indicate that the=
=20
> operation did not complete.  A good rule of thumb is that three files are=
=20
> needed to save the equivalent of a bit more than an entire field because=
=20
> slurp format approaches 6/7 of the efficiency of core-image yet it is not=
=20
> block-oriented.  Contrast this with OS/8 that is at least 1/3 LESS=20
> efficient because the format is essentially a 3-2 image of the paper-tape=
=20
> frames from the original.  Paper-tape format is rather inefficient and al=
so=20
> takes longer to load.  The decision to even use paper-tape image format i=
n=20
> OS/8 is quite flawed and was done to serve a defective agenda of the=20
> Typeset-8 group that could have been accomplished in alternate ways, yet=
=20
> this poor decision forever affects ALL OS/8 users!
>
> The /F [fast] means use the high-speed reader [defaults to the ASR reader=
=20
> on the console teletype].
>
> /R means the tape format is RIM; defaults to BIN format paper-tape].
>

> If /W is specified, then binary INPUT files should be passed because they=
=20
> will be PUNCHED to paper-tape.  /F means the high-speed punch and /R mean=
s=20
> punch in RIM format with a checksum that is also BIN-compatible.
>
> When BIN is used to load the slurp format binary files and either the /V=
=20
> or /I [if available; some systems do not support it and force /V] then th=
e=20
> program can be started at 07632.  Note:  P?S/8 supports an alias program=
=20
> called GET which just sets all of the above options.  GET /V =3D7632 is=
=20
> essentially redundant.
>
> The start at 07632 writes out the entirety of field 0 to blocks 0020-0057=
=20
> which is also known independently as the contents of the % file [blocks=
=20
> 0020-0037] and $ file [blocks 0040-0057].
>

NOTE: /! through /7 is used to specify the field of an extended memory=20
starting address; the default is field 0.  The =3Dxxxx is the 12-bit starti=
ng=20
address in the selected field.=20

