=20
>  It is important that the file is places    placeD
>
=20

> where the very first file on the device is normally found.  By an amazing=
=20
> fortuitous coincidence, the above commands can be used to transfer the co=
re=20
> image between the two systems!  From the P?S/8 standpoint, the actual dat=
a=20
> in the OS/8 file for a core image of all of field 0 [but no extended=20
> memory] corresponds to %:1 and $:1 given the correct pre-existing .SV fil=
e=20
> at the beginning of the DTA1: device.  Thus, core images of field 0 can b=
e=20
> interchanged in both directions.
>
> In SIMH, it is of course possible to interchange paper-tape binary files.=
=20
>  In fact, the P?S Extended PDP-8 Simulator [PEPS] for WINDOWS based on=20
> SIMH, there is a command to swap the files for the PTR: and PTP: devices.=
=20
>  Thus, output from one system, exit to the host, swap the files and load=
=20
> SIMH starting the other O/S.  [I would recommend this method using actual=
=20
> paper-tapes!]
>
> For source files, the following is the one most used:
>
> Assuming the DTA1: DECtape has a zeroed directory, the P?S/8 system=20
> program OS8CON defaults to exactly where the very first file would be=20
> stored.  This converts ASCII text files between the systems in both=20
> directions.
>
> The switches /0 - /7 select the P?S/8 logical unit, and the =3Dxxxx sets =
the=20
> OS/8 RECORD which can be obtained from an OS/8 DIR command.  [Note, the =
=3D=20
> uses OS/8 RECORDS not P?S/8 BLOCKS to make it easy to just plug in the=20
> number from the DIR command output.  Thus, the defaults are equivalent to=
=20
> /! and =3D0007.  [Leading zeroes are optional.]
>
> Because of the nature of the intersystem relationship, the largest file=
=20
> that can be transferred must be totally contained in the first half of th=
e=20
> maximum sized OS/8 device.  DECtapes of course are far smaller than this=
=20
> limit, and few source files are really that long.  [But some are!].
>

For an RK05 OS/8 drive if using the P?S/8 handler that corresponds, limits=
=20
the conversion to record 2047 or block 7777, but in terms of P?S/8 blocks,=
=20
this is about 2/3 of the device which is 6160 octal OS/8 records, which is=
=20
somewhat larger than 4000 records.  On some other devices, OS/8 can access=
=20
as much as 4096 records; in such a case, exactly the first half is all that=
=20
can be transferred from/to.=20

>
> The command format is:
>
> .R OS8CON, INFIL1 INFIL2 INFIL3 INFIL4 etc. > /1=3D7
>
> This transfers the concatenated contents of the P?S/8 input files and the=
n=20
> writes in OS/8 text format onto logical unit one [which in OS/8 would be=
=20
> DTA1:] starting at OS/8 record 7.  The OS/8 device should be zeroed [more=
=20
> later].
>
> To transfer the other way, use P?S/8 output files
>
> .R OS8CON, OUTFL1 OUTFL2 OUTFL3 < /1=3D7
>
> When creating output fiiles, OS8CON gives a report of how many were=20
> actually used in case too many were specified.  Error messages result if=
=20
> not enough files were used.  Up to 17 at a time are possible in a single=
=20
> command.  [There is a more complicated mechanism to use called the=20
> extended-length file, but it's a bit complicated to explain until this is=
=20
> fully understood first].
>
> The conversion "direction" is determined by whether there are P/S/8 input=
=20
> files or output files.  The syntax in turn is flexible and is a superset =
of=20
> OS/8.
>
> In OS/8 and P?S/8, the "flow" of commands can be
>
> .PROG OUT<IN
>
> But P?S/8 uniquely also allows
>
> ,PROG IN > OUT.  [Note:  The spaces can also be horizontal tabs, and are=
=20
> optional as long as the parsing makes sense, but for clarity I have used=
=20
> spaces].
>
> Thus, use either form of command that makes more sense to you.  "Hardcore=
"=20
> OS/8 users depend on the former, but newer users exposed to newer DEC=20
> systems or newer still systems from other vendors approach the command in=
=20
> the latter method.  P?S/8 is neutral on the subject and all command=20
> circumstances work either way, the user's choice.
>
> Directory fixup for OS/8.
>
> If you use OS8CON to write to OS/8 on a zeroes device, the file data is=
=20
> actually there [but cannot be accessed - yet].
>
> I personally asked for the options to fix this be added to OS/8 PIP back=
=20
> in the day [it really doesn't have much utility outside of this usage].
>
> .R PIP
>
> *DTA1:</Z   In theory, this was done already.  The data supplied by OS8CO=
N=20
> is not affected by this operation.
> *DTA1:FOO.PA</I=3D600  This creates an arbitrarily large size of a file.=
=20
>  Use a value that is plausible for the device, but error on the high side=
=20
> deliberately!
> *DTA1:FOO.PA<DTA1:FOO.PA/D  The file is looked up, and its contents=20
> becomes the input to the operation.  But the file entry is also deleted=
=20
> after that!  Thus, the read works essentially in-place.  When the interna=
l
> OS/8 Control-Z EOF is notices, the file closes at the proper length.
>
> The only requirement is that the OS/8 device is underlying=20
> storage-compatible with the relevant P?S/8 logical unit.  This is always=
=20
> true for DECtapes and LINCtapes and RX01 devices among others.
>
> P?S/8 can be configured for use with RK devices in one of four different=
=20
> configurations.
>
> 1) Entire RK packs are a logical unit.  This would allow conversions=20
> between such as RKA1: or RKA2: or RKA3: and P?S/8 unit 1 or 2 or 3.
>
> 2) Each P?S/8 logical unit is one-half of an RK pack.  This is essentiall=
y=20
> what OS/8 normally does, thus assuming P?S/8 is booted to drive 0, RKA1: =
is=20
> logical unit 2, RKA2: is logical unit 4 and RKA3: is logical unit 6.
>
> 3: Each P?S/8 logical unit is 1/4 of an RK pack.  Thus, assuming P?S/8 is=
=20
> booted to unit 0, RKA2: is unit 4 and RKB2 is unit 6.
>
> 4) Each 1/8 of an RK pack is a logical unit roughly the size of a DECtape=
.=20
>  OS/8 RKB0: is P?S/8 logical unit 4.
>
> Note that both OS/8 and P?S/8 can be booted to non-zero bootup drives=20
> using the standard RK8E EXTENDED bootstrap that is a bit longer than the=
=20
> unit-zero-onlly boot code [of two instructions].  Any configuration and=
=20
> boot drive can be derived in terms of OS8CON and core-image file=20
> compatibility.
>
