>> standard for SIMH already].=E2=80=8B  Even when converting P?S/8 TFS fil=
es, these=20
>> conventions should be maintained.
>>
>> If for some reason, P?S/8 core-images of field 0 [created for the use of=
=20
>> P?S/8 ODT, etc.] are saved by copying the % and $ files into two logical=
ly=20
>> paired TFS files. [This would only make sense in P?S/8 to defer debuggin=
g=20
>> of an active program to instead use P?S/8 for alternate purposes, and it=
=20
>> would perhaps make more sense to instead use the BSAVE command to create=
=20
>> slurp binary files instead, which does require more files but could also=
=20
>> save extended memory as well] and were somehow transferred into the Wind=
ows=20
>> host environment, perhaps these could be saved as either .SAV or perhaps=
=20
>> .SAVE.
>>
>> Since the P?S/8 SHELL design is not 100% pinned down, executable files i=
n=20
>> that environment could be called either .SAV [or .SAVE] or .EXE [or .EXE=
C].=20
>> It is conceivable both could apply, to distinguish simple executables fr=
om=20
>> more complex files that include their own internal overlay structure, a=
=20
>> capability of the basic P?S/8 system already implemented and heavily use=
d=20
>> by a few P?S/8 system programs.  [Note:  OS/8 does not support this conc=
ept=20
>> directly, but a user came up with a kludge that works, as long as you ar=
e=20
>> careful. In essence, a program is loaded into multiple fields and then e=
ach=20
>> "module" is saved safekeeping, and then a composite is created that=20
>> pretends that loading one big file into all of that memory exists.  But =
you=20
>> have to actually store the files in contiguous order on the system devic=
e=20
>> as a major restriction  Of course you have to have the faked requirement=
=20
>> memory to create this, and it will miserably fail if the files are not=
=20
>> stored in perfect contiguous order and on the system device, but it is a=
=20
>> way to get OS/8 to support what P?S/8 already has.
>>
>> This trick was invented by Richard Lary himself trying desperately to ge=
t=20
>> around the limitations of the crappy demo system that the suits convince=
d=20
>> him to use [against his better judgement].
>>
>> I had already used this available feature in the R-L Monitor System to=
=20
>> implement R-L FOCAL, which was later upgraded to P?S/8 FOCAL eventually=
=20
>> [which took advantage of the device-independent environmwnt  of P?S/8=20
>> nearly provided by OS/8 later, but with restrictions] and Richie was now=
=20
>> stuck with a turkey that was problematic.  [The idea is that some progra=
ms=20
>> have no ability to run with all of the overhead of dealing with loading=
=20
>> OS/8 non-system handlers, and as such can use the OS/8 system handler. =
=20
>> This is a complete analogy of what R-L did because ironically  both OS/8=
=20
>> and R-L have the restriction that only the system device can be accessed=
 by=20
>> the program, and the point was trying to make R-L-style core-image syste=
m=20
>> programs work on what was to become the pathetic OS/8 environment that t=
he=20
>> far better deign of the R-L environment allowed.  P?S/8 made this even=
=20
>> better because logical units actually were implemented.  If you look at =
the=20
>> system calls for OS/8, it is clear this was INTENDED, but never to be=20
>> implemented because of the structural feebility of the pathetic demo.  H=
ad=20
>> RIchie stuck to his guns, I would have gladly become the sixth memory of=
=20
>> the team, but his four immediate friends and comrades at arms rejected t=
he=20
>> demo brought on by idiotic management edict, and then I was asked and al=
so=20
>> rejected it, to instead develop R-L and here were are over 40 years late=
r=20
>> and I am doing just that!]  In any case, the P?S/8 SHELL will also suppo=
rt=20
>> this important feature that is a great structure for practical P?S/8 sys=
tem=20
>> programs, etc.]
>>
>> In essence, the P?S/8 SHELL executable format will share with OS/8 [and=
=20
>> DIAL-MS] the notion of a static load map with regards to the initial loa=
d,=20
>> but will also embrace the R-L/P?S/8 notion of an overlay structure to be=
=20
>> the best of all worlds, etc.  This is more easily done in the SHELL=20
>> environment because unlike OS/8,non-system device handlers are always us=
ed=20
>> and are allowed to be loaded into ANY available memory in 32K and are no=
t=20
>> limited to field 0 loading.  Thus, an executable must have the relevant=
=20
>> non-system handler loaded ahead of time by the SHELL keyboard monitor=20
>> outside of the load amp description, but this can be anywhere in the 32K=
=20
>> space that is appropriate.  The P?S/8 system handler is NEVER used by=20
>> executable programs. The system handler is only used by SHELL components=
 to=20
>> implement the non-system handler loading, etc. such as accessing the=20
>> non-system device handler table which is always stored in the system=20
>> device, and thus, the actual code blocks of all non-system handlers is=
=20
>> stored on the system device.  When the execution process starts, the sys=
tem=20
>> device handler is used to load the relevant non-system handler into=20
>> non-conflicting memory, and then that is called to load in the initial=
=20
>> static program image.  The file and communications area will contain all=
 of=20
>> the particulars of the execution, including the actual filename of the=
=20
>> program, a feature some other systems use to advantage,  That way, the=
=20
>> executable can start with immediate knowledge of how to access the=20
>> non-system handler to LATER load in overlay data, etc.  [Note:  Despite =
all=20
>> of the locations in field 1 the OS/8 USR routine gloms onto, it cannot b=
e=20
>> used to even attempt any of this, thus what Rickie was trying to get to=
=20
>> work, even if with restrictions.  In short, never let ignorant managers=
=20
>> with an attitude design software!
